
\chapter{Particle selection and identification\label{PartID}}


The \lam and \alam baryons and \kaon meson are neutral particles which decay into charged hadrons with a decay length, c$\tau$, of a few centimeters.  While neutral particles cannot be detected in the TPC, the charged hadron daughters can be observed and these daughters form a neutral vertex, or \vzero.  The \lam, \alam, and \kaon hadrons can be identified by reconstructing their secondary decay vertices.  Particles which decay into a \lam or \alam, such as the \myxi and the \myomega, can also be reconstructed by first reconstructing the tertiary decay vertex of the \lam or \alam decay and then reconstructing the secondary decay vertex of the \myxi or \myomega.  This method has been applied successfully by STAR to study the \lam, \myxi, and \myomega baryons and their respective antiparticles and the \kaon meson \cite{STARForwardLambdas,STARStrangenessEnhancement,STARAuAuV0Flow,STARKaonHBT,STARStrangePP200,STARHyperonScaling,STARProtonLambdaCorrelations,STARMultistrangeFlowAuAu200,STARMultistrangeBaryonAuAu130,STARParticleDependentV2AuAu200,STARKaonProductionAuAu130,STARV0AuAu130,STARLambdaAuAu130}.  Data on these particles and the decay channels used to identify them are given in \Tref{TableV0Decays}.

