The advantage of particle identification by reconstruction of decay vertices is that this method is effective at high-\pT.  Identification of \pion, K, and p by dE/dx is difficult above 1 \GeV in STAR because the bands overlap.  Studies of particles identified in STAR by vertex reconstruction are limited at high-\pT by statistics rather than the applicability of the method.  A disadvantage of particle identification by vertex reconstruction is the efficiency.  Identification of a \vzero through its decay vertex has an efficiency of about 10\% - 50\% and identification of \myxi and \myomega baryons through reconstruction of their decay vertices have an efficiency on the order of a few percent.  By comparison, the efficiency for reconstructing a charged hadron ranges from about 70\% in central \Au collisions to about 90\% in \pp collisions.


Di-hadron correlations presented in this thesis use unidentified hadrons (h), \vzeros (\lam, \alam, \kaon), or multistrange baryons (\xibary, \axibary, \omegabary, \aomegabary).  Di-hadron correlations of \vzeros, \myxi, and \myomega are only presented for \Cu at \sNNtwohundred.  The data from \Cu and \Au collisions at \sNNsixtytwo were investigated but the statistics did not allow for meaningful studies, and previous studies were done in \Au  at \sNNtwohundred \cite{YingGuo,galliard,JanaFrance,JanaWWND2007,JanaQM06,JanaHP06,JanaPANIC05,Bombara:2008zza,Guo:2005qm,Guo:2004qg,betya}.  Therefore, identification of these particles is only discussed for \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.

All particles are restricted to $| \eta | <$ 1.0 since the acceptance of the STAR TPC is limited in $\eta$ and tracks at higher $\eta$ typically have fewer hits in the TPC and worse momentum resolution due to the angle they make with the read out pads in the TPC.  All tracks used in these studies, including those of daughter particles, are required to have at least 15 hits in the TPC associated with the track.

\section{Selection of charged tracks}



Particles with \pT $>$ 1 \GeV are dominantly pions and protons, with the proton contribution as much as 50\% in central \Au collisions at \sNNtwohundred \cite{SuarezQM08}.  This means that unidentified hadrons are mostly a mixture of protons and pions.  \Fref{dDCA} shows the distribution of the distance of closest approach (DCA) of charged tracks with \pT $>$ 1.0 \GeV to the primary vertex of an event.  Tracks are restricted to a DCA $<1.0$ cm, limiting contributions from decay particles.


\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{8cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/UnidentifiedDCA.eps}}\\

  \caption[The DCA to the primary vertex for unidentified hadrons.]
  {Distance of closest approach to the primary vertex for unidentified hadrons with \pT $>$ 1.0 \GeV within $| \eta| <$ 1.0 in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.
  \label{dDCA}}
\end{figure}

The number of hits in the TPC associated with a track is also restricted to ensure the quality of the track.  Tracks with few hits are often randomly associated hits rather than those left by a charged particle and if they represent a real particle, their momentum resolution is poor.  Also, tracks from a single particle may be split and reconstructed as two tracks; requiring a minimum number of hits on a track prevents double counting of these split tracks.  \Fref{UnidentifiedNhits} shows the distribution of the number of hits associated with unidentified hadron tracks with \pT $>$ 1.0 \GeV.  The maximum number of hits possible in the TPC depends on the trajectory of the track but is usually 45 for a particle within $| \eta | <$ 1.0.  Only tracks with at least 15 tracks were accepted.

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{8cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/UnidentifiedNhits.eps}}\\

  \caption[The number of TPC hits associated with unidentified hadrons.]
  {The number of TPC hits associated with unidentified hadrons with \pT $>$ 1.0 \GeV within $| \eta| <$ 1.0 in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.
  \label{UnidentifiedNhits}}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Unidentified hadron efficiency\label{hefficiency}}
\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{8cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/CuCu200CentralEff.eps}}\\

  \caption[Effective efficiency for reconstructing unidentified hadrons in \Cu at \sNNtwohundred.]
  {The effective efficiency, including acceptance effects, for reconstructing unidentified hadrons in 0-10\% central \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  The curve is a fit to the data.
  \label{SamplehhEff}}
\end{figure}

To determine the efficiency for reconstructing tracks, the response to the particle as it traverses the STAR detector is simulated.  For unidentified hadrons, the response of the detector is simulated for pions.  There are significant contributions from protons in the data, however, the efficiency for the reconstruction of pions and protons is similar for particles with \pT $>$ 1 \GeV.  The charge which would be recorded by the detector is simulated, and the simulated charge is embedded into a real event.  The software used by STAR to reconstruct hits and tracks in real data is used to reconstruct tracks in the data-simulation hybrid.  The reconstructed track must have at least 10 of the simulated hits for the simulated track to be considered reconstructed.  The ratio of the number of reconstructed tracks to the number of simulated tracks gives the effective efficiency, including acceptance effects.  The effective reconstruction efficiency was determined for tracks meeting the quality requirements applied in the analysis and was determined as a function of \pT and centrality for each data set.  This was fit to a curve $a$ $exp((\frac{b}{p_T})^c)$.  An example of the effective efficiency for the reconstruction of unidentified hadrons in 0-10\% central \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred is shown in \Fref{SamplehhEff}.  The parameters for the fits to the efficiency as a function of \pT for each centrality bin in each data set are in \Aref{hefficiencyappendix}.


\section{\vzero reconstruction}

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{11cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/v0geometriccuts.eps}}\\

  \caption[Schematic diagram of a \vzero decay.]
  {Schematic diagram of a \lam decay showing the different parameters of a \vzero candidate's reconstructed decay vertex.  Primary vertex is abbreviated PV.
  \label{V0Schematic}}
\end{figure}

A schematic diagram of the geometry of a \lam decay is shown in \Fref{V0Schematic}.  The \kaon decay is similar except for the identity of the daughters.  This shows the different parameters describing the reconstructed decay vertex.  Restrictions of these parameters can be varied to alter the purity and the efficiency of reconstructed \vzero candidates.

To get a sample of \vzero candidates, all pairs of global tracks - those which are not forced to go through the primary vertex - are combined.  The invariant mass of the track pair is calculated assuming that they are the decay daughters of a \vzero.  The mass of a \vzero candidate is given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
m_{\Lambda} & = & \sqrt{(m_{\pi}+m_{p})^2 - (\overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}}+\overrightarrow{p_{p}})^2}\nonumber \\
m_{\bar{\Lambda}} & = & \sqrt{(m_{\pi}+m_{p})^2 - (\overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}}+\overrightarrow{p_{\bar{p}}})^2} \\ 
m_{K^0_S} & = & \sqrt{(m_{\pi}+m_{\pi})^2 - (\overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}}+\overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}})^2} \nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}
with the momentum given by 
\begin{eqnarray}
\overrightarrow{p_{\Lambda}} & = & \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}} + \overrightarrow{p_{p}} \nonumber \\
\overrightarrow{p_{\bar{\Lambda}}} & = & \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}} + \overrightarrow{p_{\bar{p}}} \\
\overrightarrow{p_{K^0_S}} & = & \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}} + \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}}. \nonumber
\end{eqnarray}

The combinatorial background in a heavy ion collision is considerably higher than in \ee or \pp collisions, and is large for high particle multiplicities.  This means that the background is greater in central collisions than in peripheral collisions.  The background is dominated by primary tracks, or those which originate from the primary vertex (PV).  Some default cuts on track quality, daughter particle identification, and geometry of the reconstructed vertex are applied to keep file sizes reasonable before finer tuning of cuts.  The list of default cuts is given in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts}.

\begin{table}
\begin{center}
\caption[Default geometric cuts for \vzeros]{Default geometric cuts for \vzero candidates.}\label{TableV0DefaultCuts}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
\hline
  	 & $p_{T}^{V^0}<3.5 $ \GeV & $p_{T}^{V^0}>3.5 $ \GeV \\ \hline
  DCA of \vzero to PV $<$&  0.8 cm & 0.8 cm \\
  DCA of daughters $<$&   0.8  cm & 0.8  cm \\ 
  DCA of daughters to PV $>$ & 0.7 cm & 0 cm \\
  Decay length $>$&  2 cm &  2 cm \\
  \nhits on daughter track $>$&  15 &   15 \\ 
  $|$ \nsigdedx $|$ $\geq$ &  3 &   3 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/DCA.eps}
  }\\

  \caption[DCA of a \vzero to the primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other]
  {DCA of \vzero to primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other for \vzero candidates between 1.0 \GeV $<$ \pT $<$ 1.5 \GeV versus the reconstructed mass of the candidate.  These were produced with the default cuts in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts} with \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  The vertical scale is logarithmic.
  \label{DCADaughtersV0s}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{DCADaughtersV0s} shows the DCA of the daughter tracks to each other and to the primary vertex versus the invariant mass of the \vzero candidate.  Plots with the parameter on the y-axis and the mass of the candidate on the x-axis easily show the difference between the signal and the background.  The signal sits near the mass of the particles and has a lower DCA on average than the background.  These plots show similar patterns for both the DCA of the daughters and the DCA of the \vzero to the primary vertex because these paramters are dominated by the resolution of the detector.  Restrictions on the DCA of the \vzero daughters to each other and to the primary vertex eliminate background efficiently, and future detector upgrades at STAR will allow better resolution of these parameters.

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/DCAPt.eps}
  }\\

  \caption[\pT dependence of the DCA of a \vzero to the primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other]
  {DCA of \vzero to primary vertex versus the reconstructed mass of the candidate for different momenta.  These were produced with the default cuts in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts} in \Cu events at \sNNtwohundred.  The vertical scale is logarithmic.
  \label{DCAK0ToPVvsPt}}
\end{figure}


\Fref{DCAK0ToPVvsPt} shows the reconstructed DCA in different bins in \pT.  A single hit in the STAR TPC has a resolution on the order of a few hundred \mum.  The resolution of the trajectory of each track depends on its momentum and on the number of hits in the TPC.  Lower momentum daughter tracks are not only more likely to have fewer hits in the TPC, but have greater energy loss, reducing both their momentum resolution and the resolution of the trajectory of the track.  This means the resolution of the DCA of the daughter tracks will be worse at low \pT.  The fact that the resolution gets better with higher momentum demonstrates that this method is better, not worse, at higher \pT.

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/DCADecay.eps}
  }\\

  \caption[DCA of \lam daughters to the primary vertex and decay length versus the reconstructed mass]
  {DCA of positive and negative \lam daughters to the primary vertex and the decay length of \lam candidates versus the reconstructed mass of the candidate.  These were produced with the default cuts in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts} with \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.
  \label{DCADaughtToPVDecay}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{DCADaughtToPVDecay} shows the distribution of the decay daughters' DCA to the primary vertex and of the decay length versus the invariant mass of \lam candidates.  Similar trends are observed for \kaon candidates.  Since most of the background comes from tracks originating at the primary vertex, most of the background has a small reconstructed decay length and the daughter tracks are close to the primary vertex.  The signal has an exponential distribution of decay lengths, meaning that much of the signal also has a small decay length.  Requiring a minimum decay lenth improves the signal to background ratio, but also significantly reduces the signal.  Likewise, requiring that the decay daughters do not come close to the primary vertex also reduces the background, but significantly reduces the signal.

The dE/dx of daughter tracks can also be used to reduce the background.  The number of standard deviations away from the center of the band is calculated and a 3$\sigma$ cut was applied by default.  \Fref{V0NSigmaExamples} shows the distributions of the number of $\sigma$ away from the center of the dE/dx band versus the mass for the daughters for \lam candidates.  The dE/dx bands from different particles begin to merge above 1 \GeV in the STAR TPC, but at higher momenta the bands begin to separate so some resolution is possible.  This is evident in \Fref{V0NSigmaExamples} because the distribution of p daughters is not centered around \nsigdedx = 0.  It is more effective for the p and \antiproton daughters of the \lam and \alam since there are more primary pions than protons produced \cite{IordanovaWWND08}.

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{14cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/NSig.eps}
  }\\

  \caption[DCA of \vzero to primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other]
  {The \nsigdedx for \lam decay daughters at different momenta versus the invariant mass.  These were produced with the default cuts in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts} with \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.
  \label{V0NSigmaExamples}}
\end{figure}

The widths of the mass peaks as a function of \pT are shown in \Fref{V0Width} and the centers of the mass peaks as a function of \pT are shown in \fref{V0Center}.  These parameters are determined from a fit of a Breit-Wigner distribution
%[2]*pow([3]/2,2)/(pow([4]-x,2)+pow([3]/2,2))
\begin{equation}
 f(M_{reco}) = N \frac{(\frac{\Gamma}{2})^2}{(M_{reco}-M_{center})^2+(\frac{\Gamma}{2})^2}
\end{equation}\label{BreitWigner}
plus a linear background to the mass peak, where $\Gamma$ is the width of the distribution, $M_{center}$ is the center of the distribution, $M_{reco}$ is the reconstructed mass, and N is the total number of particles.  The mass resolution is dominated by detector effects from the single track momentum resolution and the shape of the reconstructed mass peak is altered by the geometric cuts.  A Breit-Wigner is used because this function fits the mass peak best; the fits were only used for determining the purity of the sample.  Since the purpose of these studies was only identification of \vzeros for di-hadron correlations, the \pT dependence of the centers and widths of the mass peaks was not investigated in detail.



\begin{figure}
 \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/V0Widths.eps}}\\

  \caption[\vzero mass widths.]
  {\vzero mass widths as a function of \pT.  The solid black line indicates the PDG value and the red dashed lines indicate the PDG value $\pm$ the bin width.
  \label{V0Width}}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/V0Centers.eps}}\\

  \caption[\vzero mass peak centers.]
  {\vzero mass peak centers as a function of \pT. These were fit to $A e^{-(b/p_{T})^c}$, shown as a black dashed line.  These parameters are given in \Tref{TableV0Centers}.  The solid black line indicates the PDG value and the red dashed lines indicate the PDG value $\pm$ the bin width.
  \label{V0Center}}
\end{figure}

