\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/V0Purity.eps}}\\

  \caption[\vzero purity.]
  {\vzero purity as a function of \pT after tuning the geometric cuts.
  \label{V0Purity}}
\end{figure}


Starting with the geometric cuts given in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts}, the restrictions on the DCA of the \vzero to the primary vertex, the DCA of the daughters to each other, the DCA of the daughters to the primary vertex, the decay length, the number of TPC hits on each daughter, the \nsigdedx and the distance from the center of the mass peak for each daughter were tuned until a purity of about 95\% was reached in each centrality bin and at each momentum.  The final cuts are given in \Aref{geocutsappendix}.  These tight restrictions on the purity were used to reduce the impact of impurities on di-hadron correlations.  The purity as a function of \pT, shown in \Fref{V0Purity}, was determined by fitting the mass peak with a Breit-Wigner distribution.  The center was determined from a fit to the center as a function of \pT to $A e^{-(b/p_{T})^c}$.  The parameters of these fits are given in \Tref{TableV0Centers}.

\subsubsection{\vzero efficiency}

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/V0Efficiency.eps}}\\

  \caption[Effective \vzero reconstruction efficiency.]
  {Sample effective \vzero reconstruction efficiency for the default cuts listed in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts}.
  \label{V0Efficiency}}
\end{figure}

The effective \vzero reconstruction efficiencies were determined by embedding simulated tracks in a STAR event, as for unidentified hadrons.  The efficiency of reconstructing a \vzero in the STAR detector is limited by the single track efficiency ($\leq 0.90$) and the branching ratio (69.2\% for \kaon and 64.1\% for \lam and \alam.)  These effects combined put an upper limit on the efficiency of about 55\% before applying any geometric cuts.  As for charged hadrons, the cuts were applied to the embedded, reconstructed data, compared to the number of simulated tracks, and the ratio fit to give an efficiency.  A sample effective efficiency, including acceptance effects and the branching ratio, for the default cuts listed in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts} is shown in \Fref{V0Efficiency}.  The large jump in the efficiency at \pT = 3.5 \GeV is from the change in the cuts at \pT = 3.5 \GeV.  The fit parameters in each \pT and centrality bin are given in \Aref{efficiencyappendix}.


\section{\myxi and \myomega reconstruction}
The identification of a \myxi or \myomega through their decay vertices is a straightforward extension of \vzero identification.  Instead of \pion or p daughters, one of the daughter tracks is a \lam.  There are more parameters describing the geometry of the reconstructed decay, those describing the \lam decay vertex and those describing the \myxi or \myomega decay vertex.  The efficiency is also significantly lower than for the reconstruction of a \vzero because three tracks must be reconstructed rather than two and the branching ratios of both the \lam and the \myxi or \myomega are relevant.


\begin{table}
\begin{center}
\caption[Default geometric cuts for \myxi and \myomega]{Default geometric cuts for \myxi and \myomega candidates.}\label{TableXiDefaultCuts}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
\hline
  	 & $p_{T}<3.5 $ \GeV & $p_{T}<3.5 $ \GeV \\ \hline
  DCA of \vzero daughters &   $<$ 0.8  cm & $<$ 0.8  cm \\ %yep
  DCA of \vzero daughters to PV & $>$ 0.4 cm & $>$ 0.0 cm \\ %Neg > 0.4cm, Pos>0.4, high pt?
  DCA of \myxi to PV &  $<$ 0.8 cm & $<$ 0.8 cm \\ %yep
  DCA of \myxi daughters &   $<$ 0.8  cm & $<$ 0.8  cm \\ 
  \myxi Decay length &  $>$ 2 cm &  $>$ 2 cm \\ %>2 cm, yep
  \nhits on daughter track & $>$ 15 &  $>$ 15 \\ 
  \nsigdedx & -3 $<$ \nsigdedx $<$ 3 & -3 $<$ \nsigdedx $<$ 3 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

As with \vzero reconstruction, default cuts were applied and these cuts were tightened to reach the desired purity.  Most of the cuts applied are exactly analogous to those applied for \vzero recontruction and only cuts unique to \myxi and \myomega recontruction will be reviewed here.  \Tref{TableXiDefaultCuts} lists the default cuts used for \myxi and \myomega reconstruction.

\subsection{\myxi reconstruction\label{xireco}}

As for \vzero reconstruction, the background for the \myxi is dominated by random combinations of primary tracks.  This means that on average the distance of closest approach to the primary vertex of the \myxi will be lower on average than to the background and the DCA to the primary vertex of the \lam will be higher on average than the background.  The DCA of the \lam daughter to the primary vertex and the DCA of the \myxi candidate are strongly correlated for real \myxi. 

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{15cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/XiCutPlot2.eps}}\\

  \caption[\myxi DCA of \lam to primary vertex versus DCA of \myxi to primary vertex.]
  {The DCA of the \lam daughter to the primary vertex versus the DCA of the \myxi candidate to the primary vertex in the signal region (1315$<m_{\xi}<$1325), the background region (1270$<m_{\Xi}<$1312 and 1133$<m_{\Xi}<$1350 \MeV) and the ratio of the two for all \myxi candidates with \pT $>$ 1 \GeV in the \Cu data at \sNNtwohundred.  The line shows where the cut is applied to reduce the background in \Fref{XiMass}.
  \label{XiCuts}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{XiCuts} shows the DCA of the \lam daughter to the primary vertex versus the DCA of the \myxi candidate in the signal region (1315$<m_{\Xi}<$1325), the background region (1270$<m_{\Xi}<$1312 and 1133$<m_{\Xi}<$1350 \MeV) and the ratio of the two for all \myxi candidates with \pT $>$ 1 \GeV in \Cu collisions at \sNNtwohundred.  The strong correlation between the DCA of the \myxi candidate to the primary vertex and of the DCA of the \vzero daughter to the primary vertex was demonstrated in \cite{magali} to be an efficient way to reduce the background in the \myxi mass peak.  The line drawn in \Fref{XiCuts} shows an example of a correlated cut
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{DCA} \operatorname{of} \operatorname{V^0} \operatorname{to} \operatorname{PV} > A*\sqrt{ \operatorname{DCA} \operatorname{of} \operatorname{\Xi} \operatorname{to} \operatorname{PV}}+B\label{xicut}
\end{equation}
which can be used to eliminate background.  A tighter cut is applied by either increasing either A or B.

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{7cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/Xis/mass.eps}}\\

  \caption[\myxi mass peak with and without correlated cut]
  {\myxi mass peak before (black) and after (blue) applying the correlated cut.  The difference is shown in red.  The cut applied, DCA of the \vzero to the primary vertex $>$ 1.33 times the DCA of the \myxi candidate to the primary vertex, is shown in \Fref{XiCuts}.
  \label{XiMass}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{XiMass} shows the mass peak for \myxi candidates in \Cu at \sNNtwohundred above \pT $>$ 1.0 \GeV before applying the cut in \Eref{xicut}, after applying the cut in \Eref{xicut}, and the difference between the two.  This cut clearly eliminates mostly background.  This was one of the most efficient cuts for reducing the background.


The width and centers of the mass peaks as a function of \pT as determined from fits of the mass peaks to Breit-Wigner distributions are given in \Fref{XiCenter}.  The center was then fit as a function of \pT to $A e^{-(b/p_{T})^c}$.  The parameters from these fits are given in \Tref{TableXiCenters}.  The purity was calculated as the number of particles in the mass peak as determined from the fit divided by the total in the mass peak.  The geometric cuts were tuned until the purity was around 90\%.  The final geometric cuts are given in \Aref{geocutsappendix} and the purity is shown in \Fref{XiPurity}.  \myxi baryons are only used as trigger particles so no reconstruction efficiency is necessary.
\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{12cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/XiCenterAndWidth.eps}}\\
  \caption[Centers of \myxi mass peaks.]
  {Centers of \myxi mass peaks as determined from fits of the mass peaks to a Breit-Wigner distribution
  \label{XiCenter}}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}
\begin{center}
