\section{\myomega reconstruction}

The \myomega is more difficult to reconstruct than a \vzero or the \myxi because there are fewer \myomega baryons produced.  The default cuts are the same as those in \Tref{TableXiDefaultCuts}.  The geometry of an \myomega decay is the same as the geometry of a \myxi decay so similar cuts are applied.  The cut described in \Eref{xicut}, also works to efficiently reconstruct \myomega decay vertices.  An \myomega baryon has two decay daughters, a \lam and a \kminus, so an analogous cut can also be applied:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{DCA} \operatorname{of} \operatorname{\pi} \operatorname{to} \operatorname{PV} > C*\sqrt{ \operatorname{DCA} \operatorname{of} \operatorname{\Omega} \operatorname{to} \operatorname{PV}}+D.\label{omegacut}
\end{equation}
While this cut was not found to be useful for \myxi reconstruction, it was very useful for \myomega reconstruction.

Three different cut sets, listed in \Tref{TableOmegaDefaultCuts}, were used with varying purities.  Because the number of \myomega baryons reconstructed is small, there is a trade off between purity and the number of \myomega baryons which could be used as trigger particles for correlation analyses.  The mass peaks are shown in \Fref{OmegaMass}.  Since there are fewer \myomega baryons, the PDG value was used.  The center of the mass peak was within error of the PDG value.  The widths of the mass peaks were determined from a fit of a Breit-Wigner function to the mass peak integrated over \pT $>$ 1 \GeV.  The widths used for determining the purity were 5.6 MeV/$c^2$ for the \omegabary and 5.9 MeV/$c^2$ for the \aomegabary.

%Cut set http://rhig.physics.yale.edu/~nattrass/Research/Beginning13Sept06/17Jan/OmegaCutDetails.html
